<p>Uri is the event most San Marcos owners remember. The <a href="https://www.weather.gov/media/ewx/wxevents/ewx-20210218.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener">National Weather Service summary</a> documents the snowfall, and widespread burst pipes followed across the region. Our <a href="/freeze-damage-burst-pipe-claims-san-marcos/">burst pipe claims article</a> covers lessons from that storm.</p>

<h2>Why Freeze Claims Get Complicated</h2>
<h3>Policy conditions about heat and water supply</h3>
<p>Many property policies limit coverage for freezing of plumbing unless the owner used reasonable care to maintain heat, or shut off and drained the water when the building was unoccupied. How those words apply during a regional power failure is a fact-specific question, so the outage timeline matters.</p>
<h3>Vacant and student rental properties</h3>
<p>San Marcos has many student rentals and multifamily buildings near Texas State University. Units left empty over a winter break can fall under vacancy or unoccupancy provisions. Landlord policies often word these differently from homeowners forms.</p>
<h3>The pipe versus the water</h3>
<p>Some policies treat repairing the broken pipe differently from repairing the water damage it caused. Tear-out needed to reach the pipe may also have its own treatment.</p>
<h3>Hidden and delayed damage</h3>
<p>A slow drip inside a wall or under flooring may show up weeks later as warped wood, a stain, or a musty smell. That delay can lead to arguments about long-term seepage, which many policies exclude.</p>
<h3>Mold limits</h3>
<p>Many Texas policies cap or exclude mold coverage. Rapid drying and good records help keep a freeze claim from turning into a mold dispute.</p>

