<h2>What We Review and Document</h2>
<ul>
<li>The policy&rsquo;s freezing, vacancy, mold, and water damage provisions, plus any endorsements.</li>
<li>Where the pipe broke, what kind of pipe it was, and a plumber&rsquo;s written description.</li>
<li>Outage and temperature timing, including utility notices or texts if you have them.</li>
<li>Moisture readings, drying logs, and photos from mitigation contractors.</li>
<li>Every affected room: drywall, insulation, flooring, cabinets, trim, and paint lines that must match.</li>
<li>Damaged contents and any additional living expenses if the home was unlivable.</li>
<li>Roof, gutter, and fence damage from ice-loaded limbs, which falls under a different part of the claim.</li>
</ul>
<figure><img src="/assets/img/stock/water-damaged-ceiling-homeowners-meeting.webp" alt="Homeowners talking with an adjuster under a water-damaged ceiling" width="1536" height="1024" loading="lazy" decoding="async"><figcaption>Attic and ceiling plumbing breaks often show up first as stains and sagging drywall.</figcaption></figure>

<h2>Ice Storms: Damage Beyond the Plumbing</h2>
<p>Not every freeze claim is about pipes. During the January&ndash;February 2023 ice storm, NOAA recorded ice accumulations from about a quarter inch in Buda to about half an inch in Dripping Springs. Ice that heavy on tree limbs is a common source of property damage, and that kind of loss is closer to a storm claim than a plumbing claim:</p>
<ul>
<li>Limbs that punctured roofing or crushed gutters, carports, and patio covers.</li>
<li>Fences and outbuildings flattened by falling trees.</li>
<li>Interior water damage where a limb opened the roof and rain or melting ice followed.</li>
<li>Tree removal costs, which policies often cover only when the tree damaged a covered structure, and sometimes with a separate limit.</li>
</ul>
<p>Photograph each tree where it landed before it is cut up, and keep the tree service invoice. If wind was also part of the event, our <a href="/services/storm-damage-claims/">storm damage claims</a> page explains how mixed causes are handled.</p>

<h2>Preparing Before the Next Hard Freeze</h2>
<p>Prevention is not just good maintenance; it can also matter under the policy. Insulating attic and exterior-wall plumbing, knowing where the main shutoff is, and having a plan for vacant units all help. If you own rentals, write down what was done before each freeze, such as dripping faucets or shutting off water, so you can show reasonable care if a claim follows.</p>

<h2>How the Freeze Claim Process Works</h2>
<ol>
<li><strong>Stop the water and dry out.</strong> Shut off the main, call a plumber, and start mitigation. Keep receipts and photos.</li>
<li><strong>Report the claim.</strong> Give the insurer the date of the freeze, the date of discovery, and what was done to limit damage.</li>
<li><strong>Policy review.</strong> We read the freezing and occupancy language against the facts before the insurer&rsquo;s inspection where possible.</li>
<li><strong>Estimate and submission.</strong> We prepare a room-by-room estimate covering tear-out, rebuild, and matching.</li>
<li><strong>Follow-through.</strong> We answer questions, track payments, and request additional amounts when hidden damage is found during repairs.</li>
</ol>

<h2>Commercial and Multifamily Freeze Losses</h2>
<p>Apartment buildings, offices, and retail spaces add complexity to a freeze claim. A broken fire sprinkler line can release a large volume of water across several units at once, and tenants may have their own renters or business policies for contents. Business income losses while units or storefronts are closed may also be part of the claim. Keeping a single, organized record of which areas were affected and when repairs were completed helps every policy involved respond more smoothly.</p>

<h2>When to Call</h2>
<p>A San Marcos Public Adjuster review can make sense when a freeze claim was denied under a heat or vacancy condition, when the estimate covers drying but not rebuilding, when matching flooring or cabinets was refused, or when damage spread through several rooms or units. For leaks that are not freeze-related, see our <a href="/services/water-damage-claims/">water damage claims</a> page, and for denied claims see <a href="/services/denied-insurance-claims/">denied insurance claims</a>. Owners of multifamily buildings may also want our <a href="/services/commercial-property-claims/">commercial property claims</a> page. Start with a <a href="/free-claim-review/">free claim review</a>.</p>
